-multipass does not merge. When two devices save before iCloud syncs, iCloud keeps both versions as a conflict. The app and its extension both check for unresolved conflict versions before each save, and refuse to save while any exist. The extension saves on every registration, so it checks too: it cancels the registration and tells you to open the app, and the app shows which devices wrote the versions. So a passkey registration never silently replaces another device's change. You resolve the conflict in the app by choosing a version. The CLI does not see conflict versions, so the README tells you to edit from the CLI only while no other device is saving.
+Every save adds a new file to the package and never modifies one, as the vault-key plan's § 2.4 describes, so iCloud never holds two versions of one file to reconcile. Opening merges what each device saved, entry by entry, as the mergeable-store plan describes. So the app and its extension need no conflict screen, and changes to different entries, made on two devices while one is offline, both survive. Changes to the same entry do not: the mergeable store keeps only the version with the newer `modified`. Because a new passkey's entry is named after the site's relying party ID and joins the site's existing entry (§ 6 question 7), two devices that register for the same site while one is offline write the same entry, and only the newer registration survives. A registration on the iPhone and a password edit to that entry on the Mac collide the same way. The other change is lost without a warning, and a lost passkey leaves the site holding a public key whose private key no device has. A possible later change to the mergeable store, password history, would keep the losing version as a dated earlier version of the entry instead of dropping it; the user has suggested it and not yet decided, so this plan does not design it.
 
